---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import TextTool from "../tools/TextTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="text">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        Type a word, pick any font <strong class="text-slate-300">installed on your machine</strong>, and get it back
        as real cuttable geometry — the letters welded into one plate, with a keyring hole wherever you want it. No
        font upload, no "convert text to paths" step in another program first.
    </Fragment>

    <TextTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </TextTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Any font, no upload</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A browser will not hand over a font's outlines: a canvas can <em>draw</em> text but not say
                        where its edges are, and an SVG <code class="text-slate-300">&lt;text&gt;</code> is a promise
                        the importer has to keep, not a shape. So the word is rendered large and put through the same
                        tracer the image tool uses. Every typeface on your machine works, the browser does the kerning
                        and the ligatures, and the outline is accurate to the figure in the status bar rather than
                        exactly. Nothing is fitted across a corner: a typeface is the one thing people recognise
                        instantly — round the corners off Arial's stems and it stops being Arial — so the outline
                        follows the glyph vertex for vertex unless you ask otherwise under
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">Outline</strong>.
                        Drop a <strong class="text-slate-300">.ttf</strong>, <strong class="text-slate-300">.otf</strong>,
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">.woff</strong> or <strong class="text-slate-300">.woff2</strong>
                        on the canvas to add one that is not installed.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Cap height, not font size</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A font's em is larger than its capitals by an amount that differs per typeface, so "24 pt"
                        tells you nothing about the material. The size control here is the
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">cap height</strong>: set 20 mm and a capital comes out 20 mm,
                        in every font, whether or not the word has a descender in it.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">One piece, not a bag of letters</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Letters that do not touch come off the bed as loose shapes. The plate is traced by the same
                        code as the <a href="/LaserKit/contour/" class="text-slate-300 underline decoration-cyan-400/40 underline-offset-4 transition hover:text-cyan-300">outer contour tracer</a>,
                        so the border, <em>shrink-wrap</em>, <em>bridges</em> and the <em>taut band</em> all work the
                        same way here — widen the border until the letters weld, or join them outright.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Set round a circle</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Switch the <strong class="text-slate-300">baseline</strong> from straight to an arc and the word is
                    set round a circle instead of along a line. Each letter is <em>moved and turned as a whole</em> —
                    its outline is never bent. That distinction is the difference between type on a curve and a warped
                    picture of type: bending the outlines themselves fattens the inside of every stem and thins the
                    outside, and an O on a 20 mm badge comes out as an egg. Here the letterforms are exactly as the
                    font drew them, and only where they stand and which way they face change. They are also set round
                    the circle <em>before</em> anything is traced, in one pass, so letters that lap over each other
                    weld into one silhouette exactly as they do on a straight line — rather than staying separate
                    shapes that cancel where they cross and get cut apart through the material joining them.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    The radius is measured to the <strong class="text-slate-300">baseline</strong>, so it is the circle
                    the letters stand on rather than the one their tops reach. Set <em>over the top</em> and they stand
                    outside it; set <em>round the bottom</em> and they hang under it with their heads pointing at the
                    centre. A badge with a word above and a word below is those two runs at the same radius — export
                    the top half, switch sides, export the bottom — and because the circle is shared, the two land
                    concentric. Below about one and a half times the cap height the letters fan out like spokes and
                    stop reading as a word; the status bar says so before you cut it. Everything else in the tool works
                    the same either way: the plate still welds round the curve, and the keyring hole still goes where
                    you put it.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Letters that overlap</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Tighten the letter spacing past touching and the word becomes one silhouette — an
                    <em>rn</em> reads as an <em>m</em>. <strong class="text-slate-300">Engrave where letters
                    overlap</strong> puts the boundary back. Every letter is traced on its own and laid down left to
                    right, the way sheets of paper stack, and its outline is engraved
                    <em>only</em> where it laps over an earlier letter and is not itself covered by a later one.
                    Everywhere else the cut line already shows the shape, so a line there would be redundant and
                    would sit on top of the cut. The first letter gets nothing — there is nothing behind it to lap
                    onto. It goes out as line engraving, in green, separate from the fill.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The keyring hole</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Pick an edge, slide it along that edge, and set how far in from it the centre sits. The
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">wall</strong> is the useful part: the material kept around the hole
                    is welded into the design <em>before</em> the outline is traced, so a hole placed off the end of a
                    word grows its own lug to sit in rather than breaking out of thin air. Set it to 0 and the hole is
                    punched straight through whatever plate is already there. If it still lands off the plate, the
                    status bar says so.
                </p>
                <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Keychain</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Welded plate, letters engraved into it, a 4 mm hole on the left — the size a split ring
                            takes.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Free-standing letters</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            A 0.6 mm border and no letter operation at all: the plate's own outline <em>is</em> the
                            word, joined just enough to survive being picked up.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Stencil</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            A taut band around the whole word with the letters cut clean out of it. Mind the counters
                            — the middle of an O falls out.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Engrave only</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            No plate, no hole: just the lettering, to burn onto something you already have.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                Engraved letters export as one even-odd path in surface-engraving blue; the plate, the hole and cut
                letters all go out in cutting red. DXF has no fills, so an engraved letter goes out as its closed
                contours in the engraving colour and the alternation is left to your laser software — which is how it
                fills nested closed contours anyway. Kerf is not compensated: a 4 mm hole comes out a kerf wider, so
                drill-fit parts want a test cut first.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
